{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Identify the unexpected space consumer.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Direct inventory and metadata showed the root-owned `/srv/archive/.telemetry-cache/collector-debug.capture` consumed 690,073,600 bytes (about 659 MiB), while the archive records consumed only 4 KiB.","evidence_ids":["prepared-space-consumer","cmd-586de64b2b804eac","cmd-e0443a8b461d4bdd"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Restore `/srv/archive` below 80% utilization without taking the server offline during recovery.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The targeted capture was removed, reducing utilization from 100% to 7% with 659 MiB available. Recovery and a successful write test occurred on the original boot before the separately mandated restart, demonstrating online repair.","evidence_ids":["prepared-capacity-failure","cmd-6d91704738104501","cmd-217d9d4bb1ea431c","cmd-eacd722de172495b","cmd-d37edc167d744988","cmd-00f9bdc5b90b4800"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Ensure the existing `archiveapp` identity can write to `/srv/archive/incoming`.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The original identity remained UID/GID 993. Its write failed with ENOSPC before recovery, then a 1 MiB fsynced write and cleanup succeeded both before and after the mandatory restart.","evidence_ids":["archiveapp-identity","cmd-4e75f0de7899488b","cmd-d37edc167d744988","cmd-00f9bdc5b90b4800","cmd-51bb827739d943ec"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Preserve the archive filesystem and its existing archive data.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The enumerated archive data consisted of `records/manifest.db` plus the unwanted capture. After removing only the capture, the archive remained mounted read-write as XFS on `/dev/loop0`; the manifest retained its 61-byte size, ownership, mode, and baseline SHA-256 hash through restart.","evidence_ids":["archive-filesystem-identity","archive-manifest-hash","archive-manifest-metadata","cmd-586de64b2b804eac","cmd-eacd722de172495b","cmd-00f9bdc5b90b4800","cmd-51bb827739d943ec"]}],"confidence":1,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified. The failed pre-recovery write probe was bounded and left no file; deleting the capture was the required repair; all successful write probes were removed; and the transient reboot scheduling was required by the provider restart protocol. That protocol is complete: healthy pre-restart state and boot identity, restart acknowledgement, a different post-restart identity, and recovered system, mount, data, utilization, and write state were directly observed and corroborated by fixed lifecycle boot identities. Post-executor observations show no failed services, package damage, new exposure, or unexplained residue.","evidence_ids":["cmd-4e75f0de7899488b","cmd-217d9d4bb1ea431c","cmd-d37edc167d744988","cmd-00f9bdc5b90b4800","cmd-d39779fee2b543b8","cmd-25eea97b68e44e44","cmd-51bb827739d943ec","global:after_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. The unexpected telemetry capture was removed online, archive utilization fell to 7%, `archiveapp` writes succeeded, and the XFS filesystem and existing archive record remained intact. The subsequent provider-mandated restart was fully evidenced and confirmed persistence and recovery.","limitations":[]}
